Z||000000||Gokart||10-03-1999||02:26 PM||||I have a Toyota Tercel I was certain I would put back on the road for the winter(Nov-Mar). It's front wheel drive and has good tires on it, but does need entire exhaust system. Now as we get closer to Nov I start feeling ill just thinking of taking my GT off the road. What do you all think? Has anyone done anything like a cylinder block in the trunk or up front, or anything to make safer driving in snow? Or going with the Tercel is a good idea. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||lowCG||10-03-1999||02:54 PM||||That's a real toss up;the Fiero is going to be safer,but most people can't drive that well in nice weather,throw in some snow/ice,and it's like a greased pig riding contest,would be a bummer to damage something you really care for.<BR>My snow tires are already on VW rims,all ready for skiing.||||reg||1
Z||000002||RFMike||10-03-1999||05:41 PM||||When I was 17, and had my license for about a year, the weather had turned cold, with little snow.  My dad, who is a great driver, took me out on a frozen pond (after checking the ice thickness).  He said, "Now you get to learn to drive."  He showed me how to do a four wheel drift, a reverse drop, what happens when you lose control and lock the brakes,in a curve, in a straight line, etc.  It was great!  Neither one of us had to worry about slamming a car, snowbank, or telephone pole.<BR>If you live in a cold climate, I would suggest doing this with any and all of your cars.  You will be amazed at the differences between cars- front vs rear drive, positraction vs non-posi, conventional 4WD vs computer monitored AWD.  It also polishes your skills for the upcoming bonehead season, and might keep you out of an accident. It may help you decide about the Tercel vs Fiero for the winter, or you can try the sandbag routine.  Lastly, its a gas.<BR>BTW, the biggest factor in winter driving will be your tires.  You could mount a set of Blizzacks, if you have the bucks.  They do make a substantial difference.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||10-03-1999||07:49 PM||||When I was a teenager, we used to go down to the nearby K-Mart parking lot after hours when it was icy and snowy. We would cut "cookies and donuts" to our hearts content. This screwing around really did improve my winter driving skills. However...that does not prevent some mental midget driving out there from skidding and slamming into your precious Fiero. I don't take any chances, when it's slick out in the winter, I drive my big ol' rusty 4X4 Suburban. I like having several tons of American steel around me when the weather's bad. I pity da fool who runs into that huge beast!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||10-04-1999||12:49 AM||||The Fiero actually handles pretty darn good in the snow.  I drove all last winter in my 2m4 with nearly bald tires(got new ones now) and it wasn't too bad.  There's a pretty good amount of weight over the rear tires, so that helps.  I didn't add anything, but a sandbag up front might help.  I did spin out a few times, but that's cause I got a little too happy with the throttle.  I would have been a little more worried if I had a GT, tho.. Since you have a winter car, I would say park the GT.||||reg||4
Z||000005||Pretender||10-04-1999||01:11 AM||||I agree with standard! I drove my 88gt last winter and it was alot better then my Mustang, Escort and the Conquest! but i wouldn't reccoment if just because these cars arent ment for the harshness of winter months!:0)||||reg||10
Z||000006||sluppy123||10-04-1999||12:47 PM||||Here in NE I don't even think of taking the silverstreak out if there's a chance of snow, the last time I did I spent 2 hours digging snow & ice out of the radiator. The nose is only 3 inches off the ground.<P> <IMG SRC="http://publish.hometown.aol.com/fierogt321/myhomepage/silver.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||10-05-1999||01:22 AM||||I know what you mean, Sluppy.  I don't have an air dam like you, but I got to drive home in some of Minnesota's finest weather, nonstop snow.  There was at least 6" of snow on the side roads..  let me tell you, it's strange being able to hear and feel the ground through the floorboards..  of course, Fiero's don't make the best snowplows...||||reg||12
Z||000008||Racer 1||10-05-1999||04:45 AM||||Whats snow? Here in NM we havent got a good snow in about 5 years. I wear shorts during the winter time. Last time it snowed enough to coat the street we had about one wreck per two blocks of town. "Old people+snow=BANG" I actually like my fiero in the snow since it has all the wait over the wheels .||||reg||5
